{
  "$schema": "./node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "ryanlindsey-me",
  "account_id": "1b764d090899bf1ee61a8d1e87c10710",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-02",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat", "global_fetch_strictly_public"],

  // Day 3 Task 5 closes the day-1 open loop. /resume.pdf is the first route with
  // `export const prerender = false`, so Astro's build output flips from "static"
  // to "server", the adapter stops passing `main: undefined` to its Vite plugin,
  // and a real Worker bundle is emitted. `main` and the `ASSETS` binding are both
  // meaningful again -- and `main` is what carries `scheduled()`: without a server
  // bundle the cron handler runs under `astro dev` and is silently absent from the
  // deployed Worker.
  "main": "./src/worker.ts",
  // Day 3 Task 8 (02 §3): `run_worker_first` defaults to false, meaning a
  // request that matches a static asset is served straight from the Asset
  // Worker and NEVER reaches src/worker.ts's `fetch` at all -- confirmed
  // empirically (a debug header set unconditionally at the top of `fetch`
  // never appeared on a response for any of these paths without this
  // setting). Every one of Task 7's HTML content pages is exactly such a
  // static asset, so `Accept`-based negotiation would otherwise be dead code
  // for the one thing it exists to intercept.
  //
  // Scoped to the content and agent-signal routes only (not `true`/global), so
  // every other static asset -- `/_astro/*`, favicon, the images -- keeps the
  // fast, Worker-free asset-serving path. `*` matches across path segments
  // (Cloudflare's own docs example has `/api/*` needing `!/api/docs/*` to
  // exclude a nested path), so `/writing/*` alone covers `/writing/foo`,
  // `/writing/foo/` AND `/writing/foo.md`. Day 3 carved that last form back out
  // with two `!.../*.md` negative patterns; DAY 6 DELETED THEM, and the reason
  // is with the agent-signal block below. `/resume.json`/`.md`/`.pdf` never
  // needed a negative pattern either way: there is no `/resume/*` positive
  // pattern to catch them in the first place. The bare, no-slug forms
  // (`/writing`, `/work`) are listed explicitly rather than relied on, since
  // neither takes a markdown variant anyway and there is no reason to guess.
  "assets": {
    "directory": "./dist/client",
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    "run_worker_first": [
      "/resume",
      "/resume/",
      "/writing",
      "/writing/*",
      "/work",
      "/work/*",
      // Day 4 Task 13 (03 §1): `/mcp` has no matching entry in `dist/client`
      // (confirmed: `find dist/client -iname '*mcp*'` is empty), so today it
      // already falls through to src/worker.ts's forward with no entry here
      // -- unlike every other path above, which is listed BECAUSE it shadows
      // a real static asset. This one changes NOTHING today; it is here for
      // durability. `not_found_handling` is unset now (the plain "answer a
      // real 404" default), but `single-page-application` or `404-page`
      // would change what an UNMATCHED path does -- the Asset Worker would
      // answer it directly and src/worker.ts's `fetch` would never run,
      // exactly the "code before `handle()` is unreachable for paths it does
      // not name" trap the comment above this array already names. If that
      // ever changes, `/mcp` -- the endpoint 03 §1 calls PRIMARY -- would go
      // silently unreachable while `mcp.ryanlindsey.me` kept working: the
      // asymmetric-origin failure this whole task exists to close, reopened
      // by asset config instead of code. Do not delete this as redundant.
      //
      // What listing it here does NOT do, measured 2026-09-07 while closing
      // issue #28: it does not cost `/mcp` the `public/_headers` courtesy
      // headers. It has none (`X-MCP-Server` and `X-For-AI-Agents` are both
      // absent from a HEAD and from a POST to it), but `/resume` and
      // `/work/<slug>/` are in this same list and DO carry them -- so
      // `run_worker_first` is not what drops them. Those rules are applied by
      // the asset server to asset responses, and `/mcp` has no asset behind it.
      // The full measurement and the decision not to inject them in code are
      // written up beside the `/*` rule in public/_headers.
      "/mcp",
      // Day 5 Task 13 (04 §2, 09 §1): the same durability argument as `/mcp`
      // above, and here it guards something sharper than reachability. `/fit`
      // is on-demand, so it shadows no asset and already falls through today
      // -- these two entries change nothing now. What they insure against is
      // `not_found_handling` ever being set: the Asset Worker would then answer
      // unmatched paths itself and src/worker.ts's `fetch` would stop running
      // for them. `/fit` would keep rendering (Astro's routing lives inside
      // `handle()`), and would SILENTLY LOSE `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer` --
      // the header that keeps the scoped token in that page's URL out of the
      // `Referer` on the Turnstile script request to challenges.cloudflare.com
      // -- along with the flattening that keeps a refusal from being a
      // route-existence oracle. Both entries, because `/fit` is not matched by
      // `/fit/*`.
      "/fit",
      "/fit/*",
      // DAY 6 (06 §3) ADDED THE BLOCK BELOW AND DELETED TWO NEGATIVE PATTERNS,
      // and the deletion is the part that needs the argument, because day 3's
      // reasoning for them was correct at the time and is quoted here rather
      // than lost: routing an already-suffixed `.md` request through the Worker
      // "bought nothing but an extra hop before falling to the same asset it
      // would have been served directly anyway".
      //
      // It buys something now. A request served by the Asset Worker never
      // reaches src/worker.ts, so it cannot be classified and cannot be
      // counted -- and `/llms.txt` and the `.md` variants are not incidental
      // traffic, they are THE agent-signal routes, the ones "agents served"
      // (06 §1's headline number) exists to measure. Leaving them off this list
      // would have shipped an /ops page whose central statistic was blind to
      // the requests it was about.
      //
      // The cost is one Worker invocation per request on these paths; the bytes
      // still come from `ASSETS` either way. NOT added, deliberately:
      // `/_astro/*`, fonts and images. Those are browser sub-resources -- they
      // would multiply invocations by a factor of ten and tell us nothing an
      // agent did, since an agent fetching a markdown file requests no CSS.
      //
      // `/` IS ALSO NOT HERE, and it was in a draft of this list. The home page
      // is the busiest path on the site and the least informative one to count:
      // a request for it is not an agent signal, it is everybody. Adding it
      // would have put an invocation in front of the front page to improve a
      // "requests served" figure that /ops already labels as partial. The
      // labelling is the honest fix; the invocation was not.
      "/llms.txt",
      "/llms-full.txt",
      "/resume.json",
      "/rss.xml",
      "/feed.json",
      "/robots.txt",
      "/chat",
      "/chat/*",
    ],
  },

  "workers_dev": false,
  "preview_urls": true,
  "routes": [{ "pattern": "ryanlindsey.me", "custom_domain": true }],
  "observability": { "enabled": true },

  "d1_databases": [
    {
      "binding": "DB",
      "database_name": "ryanlindsey-me-db",
      "database_id": "3e7e8dfd-7926-4309-80ec-b583529394d3",
    },
  ],

  "kv_namespaces": [
    { "binding": "KV_CONFIG", "id": "aa1dd780da1e49eb8cf2dbf8ac9cf2b9" },
    { "binding": "KV_CACHE", "id": "c0a1106f9c9944979cd228070be21b53" },
  ],

  "r2_buckets": [
    { "binding": "R2_ASSETS", "bucket_name": "ryanlindsey-me-assets" },
    // DECLARED AND UNUSED ON THIS WORKER, deliberately, and said out loud here
    // because this is the Worker that serves anonymous traffic
    // (final-review Important 4). Nothing under src/pages or src/worker.ts
    // imports src/lib/tier/private-docs.ts -- the private tier's only reader
    // is the MCP Worker, and `/fit` reaches it over the `MCP` service binding
    // rather than reading R2 itself.
    //
    // What this comment is NOT: the partition's guarantee. That is structural
    // and lives in the type system -- `DocumentsEnv` (src/lib/mcp/documents.ts)
    // names no R2 binding at all, so a public document tool cannot reach this
    // bucket by forgetting a filter, and tests/tier-private-docs.test.ts pins
    // it with a `@ts-expect-error`. This binding is a capability the site
    // Worker holds and must never exercise, which is a weaker property than
    // the one the branch advertises, and the difference is worth knowing when
    // reading either claim. tests/pages.test.ts pins the binding list so the
    // set cannot drift without a test saying so.
    { "binding": "R2_PRIVATE", "bucket_name": "ryanlindsey-me-private" },
  ],

  "queues": {
    "producers": [{ "binding": "EVENTS", "queue": "ryanlindsey-me-events" }],
    // Day 6 (06 §3). Small batches with a short timeout, because the value of
    // these messages is TIMING -- "when a target's tools start probing, Ryan
    // knows same-hour" -- and a large batch window would trade the only
    // property they have for an email volume that is already single figures.
    //
    // The dead-letter queue is not optional-with-a-default: without one, a
    // message that fails `max_retries` is DROPPED, and the failure mode this
    // consumer is most likely to have (a misconfigured address) would silently
    // discard exactly the events it exists to deliver.
    "consumers": [
      {
        "queue": "ryanlindsey-me-events",
        "max_batch_size": 10,
        "max_batch_timeout": 30,
        "max_retries": 3,
        "dead_letter_queue": "ryanlindsey-me-events-dlq",
      },
    ],
  },

  "analytics_engine_datasets": [{ "binding": "AE", "dataset": "ryanlindsey_me_events" }],

  // Browser Run, for /resume.pdf. Measured 2026-09-06 against the installed
  // miniflare (5.20260831.0-alpha): its bundle carries a real browser-rendering
  // plugin that downloads Chrome-for-Testing and launches it locally, and ZERO
  // references to `quickAction`. So `puppeteer.launch(env.BROWSER)` works here
  // with no Cloudflare credentials, and this binding is NOT remote-only -- the
  // day-1 comment that claimed it was has been deleted rather than moved.
  // Object, not array: the schema is `{ binding, remote? }` with
  // additionalProperties: false, and it is not inherited by named environments.
  "browser": { "binding": "BROWSER" },

  // 05:17 UTC daily, the résumé-PDF job: the corpus refresh that used to share
  // this trigger moved to workers/mcp/wrangler.jsonc with the bindings it runs
  // on (see the note below). The handler is a no-op unless the résumé source
  // hash has moved (see src/lib/resume-pdf.ts), so the steady-state cost is one
  // KV read.
  //
  // 05:47 UTC daily, the retention sweep (day 6, 06 §2). A SECOND SLOT rather
  // than a second job on the same trigger, for the reason
  // workers/mcp/wrangler.jsonc already records about its own 05:32: separated
  // slots make a cron failure attributable on sight rather than by reading which
  // handler threw. Thirty minutes after the PDF job, because the two are
  // independent and there is no reason to have them contend.
  //
  // These two expressions are matched STRING FOR STRING by the `switch` in
  // src/worker.ts's `scheduled()`. Editing one without the other leaves the
  // handler matching nothing; the switch's `default` arm is what makes that
  // loud rather than a silent pair of jobs that stopped running.
  "triggers": { "crons": ["17 5 * * *", "47 5 * * *"] },

  // NO `ai` AND NO `vectorize` BINDING HERE, AND THAT IS A BUILD CONSTRAINT
  // RATHER THAN A STATEMENT ABOUT NEED. Both moved to workers/mcp/wrangler.jsonc
  // together with the corpus-refresh cron; see that file for the measurements
  // (embedding width, metric, metadata-index ordering) that used to live here.
  //
  // The `ai` binding cannot exist in THIS config, because this config is the one
  // `astro build` instantiates. @cloudflare/vite-plugin classifies `ai` as
  // "DO-NOT-USE-this-resource-will-never-have-a-local-simulator", so
  // `pickRemoteBindings` returns true for it no matter what `remote` says --
  // and `validateBindingRemoteSetting` THROWS on `remote: false`, so it cannot
  // be opted out of either. Any tooling that instantiates this Worker's
  // bindings therefore opens a remote proxy session, including
  // @astrojs/cloudflare's prerenderer, which `astro build` runs. CI has no
  // Cloudflare credentials by design (10 §2.4), so `npm run build` failed there
  // with "In a non-interactive environment, it's necessary to set a
  // CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN". A local build passed only because a dev machine has
  // wrangler OAuth -- the exact class of green-here/red-in-CI gap this repo
  // keeps finding. Confirmed by bisection: with `ai` removed and nothing else
  // changed, `astro build` completes against an isolated `HOME`.
  //
  // `vectorize` did NOT force the session (an unset `remote` resolves to local
  // simulation for it, the opposite of `ai`), so it could have stayed. It went
  // with `ai` because the two are one feature: the embedding job needs both, and
  // splitting them across two Workers would put half a job in a config that
  // cannot hold the other half.
  //
  // `browser` above stays. It is `local-and-remote` in the same plugin table, so
  // it is served by miniflare's real local browser plugin and starts no session.
  //
  // Day 4's MCP server does semantic search over this corpus, so it needed both
  // bindings regardless; the Worker that queries the corpus is now also the one
  // that fills it. CI never builds that Worker -- .github/workflows/checks.yml
  // has no reference to it -- so the conflict has nowhere left to reappear.

  // No `destination_address`: this binding may send to any address verified on
  // Email Routing, and the owner's private address must not appear in a public repo.
  "send_email": [{ "name": "EMAIL" }],

  // NO RATE LIMITER HERE, and the absence is the point rather than an
  // oversight. This config used to declare `ratelimits` with `RATE_LIMITER` at
  // namespace 1001, 60/60s -- the SAME namespace_id the MCP Worker used. Two
  // facts made it worth deleting rather than leaving:
  //
  // It was never read. `src/worker.ts` has no reference to `env.RATE_LIMITER`;
  // the only limited surface this Worker serves is `/mcp`, and that is
  // forwarded to the MCP Worker over the `MCP` service binding below and
  // limited there, on the request's own `CF-Connecting-IP` (MEASURED
  // 2026-09-08: a `User-Agent` sent to https://ryanlindsey.me/mcp arrives
  // intact in `mcp_tool_calls.user_agent`, so the forwarded request is the
  // client's, headers and all).
  //
  // And the shared namespace_id was a live trap for whoever read it next.
  // Cloudflare's docs: "Two rate limiting bindings that share the same
  // namespace_id -- even across different Workers on the same account -- share
  // the same rate limit counters for a given key." So this entry did not
  // define a second, independent 60/60s allowance; it silently joined the MCP
  // Worker's. It is not what broke #29 (sharing is the STRICTER direction, and
  // nothing here ever called `limit()`), but a future author who wired this
  // binding up would have got a bucket entangled with another Worker's and no
  // hint of it anywhere.
  //
  // The MCP Worker no longer uses `ratelimits` at all -- see
  // workers/mcp/src/rate-limiter.ts for the measurement that replaced it with
  // a Durable Object. If this Worker ever needs limiting of its own, it needs
  // the same mechanism, not this one.

  "services": [{ "binding": "MCP", "service": "ryanlindsey-me-mcp" }],

  "secrets_store_secrets": [
    // DECLARED AND UNUSED ON THIS WORKER, which is the plan's locked decision
    // written down where the binding is (day-5 plan, "Where the token is
    // verified"): *"The site Worker therefore needs no signing key; the
    // binding it already declares stays declared and unused, with a comment
    // saying so."* The comment was the half that went missing
    // (final-review Important 4).
    //
    // The reason it stays rather than being deleted: the token is verified in
    // EXACTLY ONE place, `resolveGrant` in the MCP Worker
    // (src/lib/tier/grant.ts). `/fit` treats the token as an opaque string and
    // asks that Worker, over the `MCP` service binding, whether the granted
    // surface contains `analyze_fit` -- so the page's gate exercises the real
    // boundary instead of agreeing with a second copy of it. A signing key
    // here would make a second verifier possible, which is the thing the
    // design is spending a service-binding round trip to prevent.
    {
      "binding": "RLME_TOKEN_SIGNING_KEY",
      "store_id": "3b06d2a92de642d999509352cfd3ebed",
      "secret_name": "RLME_TOKEN_SIGNING_KEY",
    },
    {
      "binding": "RLME_TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY",
      "store_id": "3b06d2a92de642d999509352cfd3ebed",
      "secret_name": "RLME_TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY",
    },
    // Day 6 (06 §3): the verified Email Routing destination high-intent
    // notifications are delivered to. A secret because it is the owner's own
    // address and must not appear in a public repo -- the SENDER is public and
    // is the `RLME_NOTIFY_FROM` var below. Read by src/lib/agent-intel/notify.ts
    // and nowhere else; its absence is a skipped send with a log line, never a
    // failed batch, so this Worker deploys and runs correctly without it.
    {
      "binding": "RLME_NOTIFY_ADDRESS",
      "store_id": "3b06d2a92de642d999509352cfd3ebed",
      "secret_name": "RLME_NOTIFY_ADDRESS",
    },
    // Day 6 (06 §1): the read-only account token /ops reads its credentialed
    // half through. Neither Analytics Engine nor AI Gateway has a binding-side
    // READ -- the `AE` binding writes and cannot query, and the gateway's
    // numbers are not exposed to a Worker at all -- so this is the one
    // credential in this repo that is not a deploy credential Cloudflare holds
    // for itself (10 §3.1). Scoped to Account Analytics Read and AI Gateway
    // Read on this account only; it can create, change and deploy nothing.
    //
    // WHICH gateway endpoint it is spent against is DETERMINED, MEASURED
    // 2026-09-11 against this account with this token, through a temporary
    // Worker route under `wrangler dev --remote` (deleted before committing):
    //
    //   - THE GRAPHQL DATASET `aiGatewayRequestsAdaptiveGroups` ANSWERS 200
    //     with the spend, request and cache-hit numbers /ops publishes. POST to
    //     `https://api.cloudflare.com/client/v4/graphql`. This is the one
    //     `readSpend` in src/lib/ops/analytics.ts now calls.
    //   - THE REST ROUTE `/accounts/{account}/ai-gateway/gateways/{id}` ANSWERS
    //     403 `{"code":10000,"message":"Authentication error"}`. Ruled out for
    //     this token; do not reach for it again on the assumption that the
    //     GraphQL query is the awkward way round.
    //
    // The Analytics Engine half was measured in the same run: a POST to
    // `/accounts/{account}/analytics_engine/sql` answering
    // `{ meta, data, rows, rows_before_limit_at_least }`, with `UInt64` columns
    // as JSON strings and `Float64` as numbers. src/lib/ops/analytics.ts
    // transcribes the recorded envelopes at more length -- the probe's raw
    // output is in gitignored working notes, so those comments and this one are
    // the record, and the two are meant to be edited together.
    //
    // Read by src/lib/ops/analytics.ts and nowhere else. Its absence is a
    // labelled "not configured" section on /ops, never a zero and never a
    // failed render, so this Worker deploys and serves correctly without it --
    // which is the state every test in this repo runs in.
    {
      "binding": "RLME_ANALYTICS_TOKEN",
      "store_id": "3b06d2a92de642d999509352cfd3ebed",
      "secret_name": "RLME_ANALYTICS_TOKEN",
    },
  ],

  // SITE_ORIGIN is what the Browser Run job navigates to. It is deliberately a
  // var rather than something derived from `request.url`: Wrangler's
  // `--infer-origin-from-routes` defaults to true, so with the custom-domain
  // route above, `request.url` reads as https://ryanlindsey.me/... even under
  // local dev -- deriving the render URL from it would make local dev silently
  // render production. The test harness overrides it (tests/workers.ts); under
  // `astro dev`, override it in .dev.vars rather than editing this value.
  "vars": {
    "RLME_AI_GATEWAY_ID": "ryanlindsey-me",
    // The account this Worker's Analytics Engine dataset and AI Gateway live
    // in, for the two REST reads /ops makes (src/lib/ops/analytics.ts). A
    // PUBLIC identifier (10 §2.6) and already the top-level `account_id` in
    // this same file -- it is repeated here because `account_id` is wrangler's
    // own field and is not exposed to the Worker at runtime.
    "RLME_ACCOUNT_ID": "1b764d090899bf1ee61a8d1e87c10710",
    // The sender. PUBLIC -- it is an address on this site's own domain and it
    // appears in the `From` header of every notification. The DESTINATION is
    // the secret half and is never here (see `secrets_store_secrets` above);
    // `send_email` deliberately declares no `destination_address`, which is
    // what lets the binding send to whatever verified address that secret names.
    "RLME_NOTIFY_FROM": "notifications@ryanlindsey.me",
    "SITE_ORIGIN": "https://ryanlindsey.me",
    // PUBLIC (10 §2.6), and here rather than in a `.env` for two reasons: the
    // one page that uses it is on-demand rendered, so it reads this at request
    // time from `Astro.locals.runtime.env`; and this is where the account id
    // and gateway id already live, which is the right neighbourhood for an
    // identifier that ships in page HTML anyway. The SECRET half is
    // `RLME_TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY` in Secrets Store and is never here.
    //
    // Named without the `PUBLIC_` prefix 10 §2.6 suggests: that prefix is
    // Astro's convention for build-time env, this is a Worker var, and 10
    // §3.2's own rule is `RLME_<PURPOSE>` with no prefix.
    "RLME_TURNSTILE_SITEKEY": "0x4AAAAAAElhnY8ov3OYHN8m",
  },
}
